deploying.md: the registrar is Network Solutions, and that is the unlock
One section in `docs/deploying.md`, recording a fact that cost a `whois` lookup
and decides what is possible.

## The finding

| | |
|---|---|
| Registrar | **Network Solutions, LLC** |
| Nameservers | `ns4.wixdns.net`, `ns5.wixdns.net` |
| Registered | 2014-06-19, expires 2027-06-19 |
| Status | `clientTransferProhibited` — transfer lock |

**Wix holds the DNS. It does not hold the registration.** Nothing in this
repository said so, and the difference is the whole ballgame: Wix does not
permit changing the nameservers of a domain *registered with Wix*, and this is
not one of those. **The nameservers can be changed at Network Solutions today**,
with no transfer and nobody's permission.

## Why it is worth a section rather than a chat message

Three things here are easy to get backwards, and getting them backwards looks
like being blocked when you are not:

- **Moving nameservers is not transferring the registration.** Separate acts, at
  separate companies. Only the first makes Cloudflare authoritative.
- **`clientTransferProhibited` blocks a registrar transfer, not a nameserver
  change.** It only matters if the domain is going to move registrar.
- **Cloudflare Registrar requires Cloudflare nameservers first.** So a
  nameserver move is a *prerequisite* for that transfer, not a consequence of
  it — and it is optional regardless. Nothing about serving this site or member
  subdomains needs the registration to move.

Also notes that keeping the apex on Wix after a nameserver move is Wix's
documented **pointing** method, with Wix's own caveat: once connected by
pointing, Wix will not help manage records it no longer hosts.

## What it does not claim

The existing paragraph says Cloudflare issues a real certificate for
`new.fcpublicmedia.org` while Wix is authoritative. **I left that alone** — it
was measured here and I have no evidence against it, but I could not work out
from the repository *which* Cloudflare mechanism is producing that certificate.
If it turns out to be a Cloudflare for SaaS custom hostname created by hand,
that entry will want removing after a nameserver move, because the subdomain
becomes an ordinary record in our own zone at that point. Worth a look while in
the dashboard; not worth a guess in the docs.

